## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime-extensible pipelines for cross-cutting concerns without touching business logic. It probes understanding of the Decorator/Chain patterns, module registries, and dynamic composition under real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Decorator/Middleware pattern for composition
- Runtime registry for plugins by name
- Per-route configuration and execution order
- Async compatibility and fault isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal TypeScript-like skeleton
interface Context { /* request-scoped data */ }
interface Response { /* response data */ }

interface Concern {
  handle(context: Context, next: () => Promise<Response>): Promise<Response>;
}

interface ConcernFactory {
  create(): Concern;
}

class Registry {
  private concerns = new Map<string, ConcernFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: ConcernFactory): void {
    this.concerns.set(name, factory);
  }

  get(name: string): ConcernFactory | undefined {
    return this.concerns.get(name);
  }
}

class Pipeline {
  constructor(private registry: Registry) {}

  build(
    concernNames: string[],
    coreHandler: (context: Context) => Promise<Response>
  ): (context: Context) => Promise<Response> {
    const concerns = concernNames
      .map(name => this.registry.get(name)?.create())
      .filter(Boolean);

    return concerns.reduceRight(
      (next, concern) => (context) => concern.handle(context, next),
      coreHandler
    );
  }
}
```

## How It Works

1. **Registration**: Concerns register factories by name in the Registry
2. **Configuration**: Routes specify concern names in configuration
3. **Resolution**: Pipeline looks up each name and creates Concern instances
4. **Composition**: Chain builds by wrapping concerns around core handler
5. **Execution**: Each concern can modify context, call next(), or short-circuit

This design enables runtime extensibility while maintaining clean separation between cross-cutting concerns and business logic.

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes the ability to design a runtime-extensible, production-ready middleware pipeline that supports dynamic loading, configurable ordering, and safe concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- **Registry Pattern**: Enables dynamic middleware registration by name at runtime
- **Pipeline/Decorator Pattern**: Composes middleware into an ordered execution chain
- **Per-Request Isolation**: Each request gets its own context instance, preventing state leakage
- **Safe Parallelization**: Independent middleware can execute concurrently when dependency ordering allows
- **Short-Circuiting**: Middleware can halt pipeline execution by returning early

## Code Example
```typescript
type NextFn = () => Promise<void>;

interface Middleware {
  name: string;
  handle(ctx: Context, next: NextFn): Promise<void>;
}

class MiddlewareRegistry {
  private middleware = new Map<string, Middleware>();

  register(name: string, factory: () => Middleware): void {
    this.middleware.set(name, factory());
  }

  get(name: string): Middleware | undefined {
    return this.middleware.get(name);
  }

  createPipeline(names: string[]): Middleware {
    const chain = names.map(n => this.middleware.get(n)).filter(Boolean);
    return {
      name: 'pipeline',
      async handle(ctx, next) {
        let idx = 0;
        const run = async (): Promise<void> {
          if (idx < chain.length) {
            await chain[idx++].handle(ctx, run);
          } else {
            await next();
          }
        }
        await run();
      }
    };
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new MiddlewareRegistry();
registry.register('auth', () => ({ name: 'auth', async handle(ctx, next) { /* auth logic */ await next(); } }));
registry.register('logging', () => ({ name: 'logging', async handle(ctx, next) { /* logging */ await next(); } }));

const pipeline = registry.createPipeline(['auth', 'logging']);
await pipeline.handle(requestContext, () => handleRequest(requestContext));
```

## Trade-offs: Pipeline vs Chain of Responsibility

| Aspect | Pipeline | Chain of Responsibility |
|--------|----------|------------------------|
| **Control Flow** | Centralized composition; executor controls ordering | Decentralized; each handler decides whether to pass on |
| **Flexibility** | Easier to reorder, split, or reuse stages | More dynamic routing decisions at each node |
| **Error Handling** | Simpler to implement centralized error boundaries | Errors must propagate explicitly through handlers |
| **Debugging** | Clear execution path through pipeline | Harder to trace; handlers may skip chain |
| **Parallelization** | Natural fit for parallel branches in some implementations | Less suited; handlers typically execute sequentially |

**When to prefer Pipeline**: Fixed ordering, clear composition, easy configuration per endpoint.

**When to prefer Chain of Responsibility**: Dynamic routing, conditional processing, when handlers need autonomy over flow control.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a pluggable, runtime-extensible command system for a GUI, mirroring IDE plugins like VS Code.

## Key Concepts
- Command pattern and type-safe interfaces
- Runtime plugin/registry architecture
- Conflict resolution strategies (override, error, namespacing)
- Lazy canExecute evaluation and cancellation propagation

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  execute(): Promise<void> | void;
  shortcut?: string;
  category?: string;
  canExecute?: () => boolean;
}

type ConflictStrategy = 'override' | 'error' | 'namespace';

class CommandRegistry {
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>();
  private strategy: ConflictStrategy = 'override';

  constructor(strategy: ConflictStrategy = 'override') {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  register(command: Command): void {
    if (this.commands.has(command.id)) {
      if (this.strategy === 'error') {
        throw new Error(`Command "${command.id}" already registered`);
      }
      if (this.strategy === 'namespace') {
        command.id = `${command.category || 'default'}:${command.id}`;
      }
    }
    this.commands.set(command.id, command);
  }

  unregister(id: string): boolean {
    return this.commands.delete(id);
  }

  get(id: string): Command | undefined {
    return this.commands.get(id);
  }

  getAll(): Command[] {
    return Array.from(this.commands.values());
  }

  getByCategory(category: string): Command[] {
    return this.getAll().filter(c => c.category === category);
  }

  async execute(id: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
    const cmd = this.commands.get(id);
    if (!cmd) throw new Error(`Command "${id}" not found`);
    if (cmd.canExecute && !cmd.canExecute()) {
      throw new Error(`Command "${id}" cannot execute`);
    }
    await cmd.execute();
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new CommandRegistry();

registry.register({
  id: 'save',
  title: 'Save Document',
  category: 'File',
  shortcut: 'Ctrl+S',
  canExecute: () => hasUnsavedChanges,
  execute: async () => { /* save logic */ }
});

registry.register({
  id: 'git:commit',
  title: 'Git Commit',
  category: 'Version Control',
  execute: () => { /* commit logic */ }
});
```

## Key Design Decisions
- **Lazy evaluation**: canExecute is invoked immediately before execution, enabling dynamic state checks
- **Cancellation**: AbortController/signal support allows long-running commands to be cancelled
- **Flexible conflict resolution**: Strategy pattern accommodates different plugin ecosystems
- **Type safety**: TypeScript ensures commands expose required properties at compile time

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a runtime-extensible plugin system with clear interfaces, error handling, and concurrency controls for a distributed task system.

## Key Concepts

- Command pattern with pluggable handlers
- Runtime registry / plugin loader
- Retry policy with backoff
- Global rate limiting and concurrency control
- Runtime loading without restart

## Code Example

```typescript
interface CommandHandler<T> {
  execute(payload: T): Promise<void>;
}

type Registry<T> = Map<string, CommandHandler<T>>;

interface RetryPolicy {
  maxRetries: number;
  backoffMs: number;
}

class Dispatcher<T> {
  private registry: Registry<T> = new Map();
  private policies: Map<string, RetryPolicy> = new Map();
  // pseudo semaphore for rate limiting
  async dispatch(name: string, payload: T): Promise<void> {
    const handler = this.registry.get(name);
    if (!handler) throw new Error('Handler not found');
    const policy = this.policies.get(name) ?? { maxRetries: 0, backoffMs: 0 };
    let attempt = 0;
    while (true) {
      try {
        await handler.execute(payload);
        return;
      } catch (e) {
        if (attempt >= policy.maxRetries) throw e;
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, policy.backoffMs));
        attempt++;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

